<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Rostov</h1><p class="st-meta">1207 CE – 1474 CE · capital Rostov</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An appanage principality based on the ancient town of Rostov on Lake Nero, gradually bought out by Moscow.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> is one of the oldest towns of north-eastern Rus', first mentioned in 862 in the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merya">Finnic Merya</a>, and was long the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. In 1207 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-the-big-nest">Vsevolod the Big Nest</a> gave it to his eldest son Konstantin, and his descendants ruled it as a principality within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal</a> lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> was a religious and commercial centre with a long tradition of chronicle writing. Its prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasilko-konstantinovich">Vasilko Konstantinovich</a> was captured and killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-sit-river">Battle of the Sit</a> in 1238. Later the principality was split between two lines, each ruling half the town, and it became a minor power between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and Tver.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow's</a> influence grew through the 14th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> princes sold the first half of their rights to Moscow in the 15th century. In 1474 the last princes sold the remaining half to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>. The town remained a great church centre, and its kremlin was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona-sysoyevich">Metropolitan Jonah</a> in the 17th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-rostov.jpg" alt="Principality of Rostov" width="320" height="283"><figcaption>Image: Goran tek-en, following request by and knowledge from Veverve · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Principality_of_Vladimir-Suzdal_in_1237_(en).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1207 CE – 1474 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rostov</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Konstantin Vsevolodovich, Vasilko Konstantinovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rostov Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1340&amp;focus=principality-of-rostov" aria-label="Principality of Rostov on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5558 267 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Rostov lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5878 523L5878 523M5878 523L6352 637M5878 523L5820 533M5878 523L5854 483M5878 523L5822 486M5878 523L5792 423"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5878 523h0M6352 637h0M5820 533h0M5854 483h0M5822 486h0M5792 423h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5878 523h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5878 523h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1340&amp;focus=principality-of-rostov">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
